{
  // Gate CI on HIGH and CRITICAL severity advisories only.
  //
  // WHY NOT all severities: `npm audit` reports advisories against the entire
  // dependency graph regardless of reachability, and this tree's moderate/low
  // advisories fall into three unactionable classes:
  //   1. PHANTOM — `dompurify` is pulled by monaco-editor, which VENDORS its own
  //      copy (`esm/vs/base/browser/dompurify/dompurify.js`) and imports THAT,
  //      not the npm package. The npm `dompurify` is never executed; there is no
  //      reachable vulnerability and nothing to fix. The advisory DB mints new
  //      DOMPurify bypass advisories continuously (8 -> 16 in days, 17 as of
  //      2026-07-27), so a per-GHSA allowlist is an unwinnable treadmill.
  //   2. NOT WORTH THE FIX — transitive advisories that a root `overrides` entry
  //      COULD reach, but only by forcing a major version into a parent that
  //      pins an exact older one. Root overrides DO reach nested transitives of
  //      workspace dependencies (proven 2026-07-27, PR #150 -- an earlier note
  //      here claiming otherwise was wrong), so "no fix exists" is almost never
  //      the real reason. State the actual cost/benefit instead. NO current
  //      members -- kept as guidance for classifying future entries.
  //   3. UNREACHABLE — the vulnerable code path is not imported by anything this
  //      repo actually uses (see @hono/node-server below).
  //
  // Gating on all severities produced constant red CI that blocked unrelated PRs
  // and trains force-merging -- a worse security posture than green CI that fails
  // only on what is actually severe and actionable. High/critical advisories are
  // rare, warrant breaking the build, and are usually fixable. Current tree:
  // 0 high package findings, 0 critical, and no allowlist entries. Every
  // remaining moderate traces to one of the two entries enumerated below
  // (dompurify via monaco-editor, @hono/node-server); run `npm audit` to
  // review them.
  //
  // KNOWN ACCEPTED moderate/low advisories (informational; below the gate, so not
  // listed as allowlist entries -- re-triage on major dependency upgrades):
  //   - dompurify (via monaco-editor): PHANTOM, see above. 17 distinct GHSAs as
  //     of 2026-07-27. No fix possible/needed.
  //   (@opentelemetry/core GHSA-8988-4f7v-96qf was accepted here as class 2 and is
  //   now RESOLVED -- lighthouse@13.4.1 dropped its bundled @sentry/node entirely,
  //   removing all 11 @opentelemetry/instrumentation-* packages and all 12 nested
  //   @opentelemetry/core@1.30.1 copies. One hoisted @opentelemetry/core@2.10.0
  //   remains, from this repo's own @sentry/nextjs. Landed via PR #153.)
  //   - @hono/node-server: GHSA-frvp-7c67-39w9 (`<2.0.5`, path traversal in
  //     `serve-static`). UNREACHABLE, class 3 above, re-verified 2026-07-27 against
  //     @modelcontextprotocol/sdk@1.30.0. Reached via
  //     packages/mcp -> @modelcontextprotocol/sdk -> @hono/node-server@1.19.14.
  //     Only the SDK's Node-specific `server/streamableHttp.js` transport imports
  //     @hono/node-server; `packages/mcp/src/http.ts` imports
  //     `server/webStandardStreamableHttp.js`, which contains ZERO hono references.
  //     The vulnerable serve-static path is never loaded. Fixing would force a major
  //     (>=2.0.5) against the SDK's declared `^1.19.9`. REVISIT if packages/mcp ever
  //     switches to the Node `streamableHttp` transport, or when the SDK widens its
  //     range -- re-check with `grep -rl "@hono/node-server"` against the installed SDK.
  //
  // If a HIGH/critical advisory ever lands that is itself phantom/unfixable,
  // allowlist that ONE advisory here by GHSA id with a dated reason -- a rare,
  // deliberate exception, not a standing treadmill.
  "high": true,
  "allowlist": []
}
